#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hestia-video — declarative action-script runner for hestia-shot's record mode.

Companion to hestia-shot/hestia-type (docs/video-capture-design.md): turns a
small YAML script (an ordered list of typed/keyed actions, grouped into
labelled beats for readability) into ONE hestia-type invocation wired up as
hestia-shot's HESTIA_HOOK, then runs hestia-shot to record it. Removes the
need to hand-assemble HESTIA_HOOK shell strings for every demo.

Deliberately narrower than the design doc's original storyboard-compiler
plan: no narration/TTS, no ffprobe duration math, no audio mux. Silent
capture only — see the doc's phase notes for why. Beats have no timing
significance of their own; pacing is entirely the actions' explicit pause:
entries plus hestia-type's own per-character timing.

Usage:
  hestia-video SCRIPT.yml -o out.mp4 [OPTIONS]

Script format (YAML):
  title: "optional, documentation only"
  scene: app            # a hestia-shot scene (default: app)
  scene_arg: bash        # hestia-shot's $2 for this scene (default: bash)
  res: 1280x720           # optional, overrides HESTIA_RES
  variant: dark            # optional, overrides HESTIA_VARIANT
  wpm: 200                  # optional, default below (see DEFAULT_WPM)
  jitter: 0.35                # optional, hestia-type default
  seed: 1                       # optional, default below — set for a
                                  # reproducible take while iterating
  drop_compensation: opener       # optional, hestia-type default
  typo_rate: 0.03                  # optional, hestia-type default — chance
                                     # per typed letter of a plausible
                                     # adjacent-key slip, caught and
                                     # corrected immediately. 0 disables.
  line_pause_ms: [250, 800]         # optional, default above — a "thinking
                                      # about the next line" beat inserted
                                      # after every newline INSIDE a text:
                                      # block (not after an explicit key:
                                      # Return the script wrote itself)
  workdir:                          # optional: files staged into a FRESH
    hello.c: |                       # temp dir before recording — the
      ...                             # scene's cwd (e.g. the app scene's
                                        # bash starts there). Omit for a
                                        # genuinely empty starting directory.
  beats:
    - label: "write hello.c"           # optional, dry-run/organisation only
      actions:
        - text: "vim hello.c"           # typed via hestia-type
        - key: Return                    # a single named key (press+release,
                                           # instantaneous)
        - hold: {key: Right, ms: 600}     # press, hold for ms, release -- for
                                           # a program polling LIVE key state
                                           # (SDL_GetKeyboardState and
                                           # similar), where key:'s
                                           # instantaneous tap never registers
                                           # as "held". See hestia-type's own
                                           # docstring for the wtype -P/-p
                                           # mechanics.
        - pause: 500                      # milliseconds, no keystroke
        - text: |                          # embedded newlines become key:Return
            multi
            line
            text
        - comment: "why this line matters"  # C-only: typed as " /* ... */"
                                              # at the current cursor, held
                                              # long enough to read, then
                                              # BackSpace'd away — the file
                                              # ends up byte-identical to
                                              # not having this action at
                                              # all. Typing ramps faster for
                                              # longer text (read-while-
                                              # typed, not just during the
                                              # hold) — see comment_pacing().
        - comment:                           # block form: its own line(s)
            text: |-                          # above the code it explains,
              first line of the explanation    # deleted with dd instead of
              second line                       # BackSpace. lines_up is how
            lines_up: 3                          # many lines above the
                                                   # CURRENT cursor the code
                                                   # being explained starts —
                                                   # read it off relativenumber
                                                   # (on in the rig for this)
                                                   # while writing the scene.
            hold_ms: 3000                          # optional override of the
                                                     # auto-computed hold

Each beat's actions are concatenated across ALL beats into ONE hestia-type
call — never one call per beat/action (hestia-type's own docstring explains
why: splitting the invocation reintroduces its nested-rig input-drop quirk).

Options:
  -o, --output PATH     output file (.mp4/.mkv/.webm); default: SCRIPT's
                         basename with .mp4, in the current directory
  --dry-run              print the resolved HESTIA_HOOK + hestia-shot
                         invocation and a rough duration estimate; do not
                         spin up the rig
  --keep-workdir          don't delete the staged temp working directory
                          after the run (default: deleted on success)
  --res WxH                override the script's res
  --variant dark|light      override the script's variant
  --seed N                    override the script's seed
  --locale LOCALE               override the script's locale (default
                                 en-GB) — see comment:'s locale map below

Locales: a comment:'s text may be a plain string (typed identically for
every locale — the default, and the only form any pre-2026-08-19 script
uses) or a mapping of locale code to string:

  - comment:
      text:
        en-GB: "pulls in the standard I/O header, so printf is available"
        el-GR: "..."
      # falls back to the en-GB entry for any locale not yet present —
      # translation work stays independently pausable per the project's
      # own broader locale-cadence decisions, this never blocks a render

CODE is never translated (typed identically regardless of locale — C
syntax/keywords aren't language-dependent) — only comment: text varies.
Reuse the chapter's own established vocabulary from apps/thecodingidiot/
content/TRANSLATION.md and the real translated .mdx files, don't
re-translate independently, same practice tools/manim's locale scenes
already follow. Rendered output naming isn't auto-derived (-o stays
explicit) — follow tools/manim's own <basename>-<locale> convention by
hand, e.g. f04-your-first-program-el.mp4.

`res:` for a `scene: game` recording MUST match the target binary's own
compiled window size, not an arbitrary "smaller file" choice — a real bug
hunted down and fixed 2026-09-01 while adding hold: (below) for
thecodingidiot's g02a/g02b platformer clips. Recording g02a (a fixed
800x608 SDL2 window per its own map.h) at res: 640x480 — the size c06's
depthvis scene uses — produced a video that looked "correct" at a glance
(the window mapped, fullscreened, showed the sky-blue clear colour) but
never rendered a single tile or sprite: EVERY frame across MULTIPLE
different recordings was a flat, single-colour rectangle, confirmed by
histogramming each frame's actual pixel colours, not by eyeballing.
Re-recording at res: 800x608 (matching WINDOW_W/WINDOW_H) fixed it
completely — real ground/platform/player colours appeared immediately.
Root cause, isolated by comparing against c06's own depthvis (which DOES
render correctly at a mismatched-from-nothing 640x480, because that's
ITS OWN native size): depthvis draws with SDL_RenderFillRect (solid
colour, no texture) while g02a/g01b/g02b draw via IMG_LoadTexture +
SDL_RenderCopy — texture-blit rendering, under this rig's headless
pixman/software-GL stack, silently produces nothing but the clear colour
when the compositor fullscreens the window into an output size that
doesn't match the surface's own committed buffer size (g01b's own scene
happens to already use its native size, which is why this went
undetected until g02a's higher native resolution exposed it). Lesson:
for ANY new `scene: game` recording, read the target binary's own
window-creation call (or its equivalent constant) for the real window
size and set `res:` to exactly that — don't copy another scene's `res:`
value on the assumption "small is fine," and don't trust that a
recording "looks like the game" from the window mapping/fullscreening
alone — histogram a few real frames before trusting a take, the same
discipline this file's own README already applies to typing scenes'
output files.
"""
import argparse
import os
import random
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import textwrap
import shutil
from pathlib import Path

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    sys.exit("hestia-video: PyYAML is required (pip install pyyaml)")

DEFAULT_WPM = 200  # slower/more deliberate than hestia-type's own 280 default —
                    # this tool is for teaching demos, not a confident-typist stunt
DEFAULT_SEED = 1   # reproducible by default; re-running the same script while
                    # iterating on a take should reproduce the same timing
DEFAULT_LOCALE = "en-GB"  # matches the project's own base-locale convention
                    # (content/en-GB/ is what every other locale falls back
                    # from) — a comment:'s locale map falls back to this key
                    # when the target locale isn't present yet
DEFAULT_LINE_PAUSE_MS = (250, 800)  # a beat to "think about the next line" —
                    # hestia-type has no concept of a line (it only ever sees
                    # one text: chunk at a time, already split at newlines by
                    # flatten_actions below), so line-level pacing is this
                    # tool's job, not hestia-type's

# comment: pacing — a long explanation is being READ while it's typed, not
# just during the hold after (owner, 2026-08-19), so it doesn't need the
# same deliberate pace as code being composed. Numbers are starting points,
# same as every other pacing constant here — tune after watching a render.
COMMENT_WPM_MAX = 340
COMMENT_WPM_PER_CHAR = 0.5
COMMENT_HOLD_BASE_MS = 1200
COMMENT_HOLD_PER_WORD_MS = 150
COMMENT_HOLD_MAX_MS = 4000

# Auto-fallback: an inline comment: that would overflow the terminal's
# actual column width gets rendered as a block instead (owner, 2026-08-19
# — nvim soft-wraps an overflowing inline comment, which stays readable
# and is harmless since it's deleted afterward, but "would be nicer if it
# didn't happen"). MEASURED_USABLE_COLS is real `:echo winwidth(0)`/
# `&numberwidth` probes rendered through the actual rig at each listed
# `res:` — not estimated. Column count is NOT linear in pixel width
# (confirmed by measuring 5 resolutions, not just interpolating from one):
# window chrome eats a roughly fixed PIXEL amount, so a naive single-point
# scale (PX_PER_COL below) systematically UNDERSHOOTS at larger sizes —
# e.g. it predicts 100 cols at 1024x768 where the real number is 104.
# Exact resolutions get their real measured value; PX_PER_COL/GUTTER_COLS
# are a rough fallback for anything unmeasured, and any new `res:` a scene
# actually uses should get a real probe added here rather than trusting
# that fallback — see docs/video-capture-design.md §18/§19 for the probe
# method.
MEASURED_USABLE_COLS = {
    "640x480": 61,
    "768x576": 76,
    "800x600": 79,
    "832x624": 83,
    "1024x768": 104,
}
PX_PER_COL = 640 / 65
GUTTER_COLS = 4
INLINE_OVERFLOW_MARGIN = 2  # small safety buffer, not an exact-fit cutoff


def resolve_repo():
    """Same resolution order as hestia-shot itself (user/bin/hestia-shot):
    HESTIA_REPO env override, else derive from this script's own location
    (works from the deployed ~/.local/bin symlink too), else the known
    shared clone.
    """
    repo = os.environ.get("HESTIA_REPO")
    if repo:
        return Path(repo)
    candidate = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent
    if (candidate / "user").is_dir():
        return candidate
    return Path("/srv/devshare/hestia")


def stage_rig_home(root, repo, variant):
    """A recording-profile $HOME — NOT the invoking user's real dotfiles.
    docs/video-capture-design.md §11: a clip rendered under the real
    deployed ~/.bashrc/~/.vimrc leaks operator identity (PS1 shows the
    real username) and vim's filetype indent plugin mangles literal typed
    whitespace. Staging a dedicated, versioned bashrc/vimrc as $HOME for
    just the recorded shell's child process fixes both, identically
    regardless of which real user runs hestia-video — same isolation
    hestia-shot itself already applies to waybar/alacritty theme configs,
    just extended to bash+vim for this scene.
    """
    rig = root / "rig-home"
    rig.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    video_rig = repo / "user" / "video-rig"
    shutil.copy(video_rig / "bashrc", rig / ".bashrc")

    vimcolors = rig / ".vim" / "colors"
    vimcolors.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    shutil.copy(repo / "user" / "vim" / "colors" / "hestia.vim", vimcolors / "hestia.vim")
    shutil.copy(video_rig / f"vimrc-{variant}", rig / ".vimrc")

    nvim_cfg = rig / ".config" / "nvim"
    nvim_colors = nvim_cfg / "colors"
    nvim_colors.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    shutil.copy(repo / "user" / "vim" / "colors" / "hestia.vim", nvim_colors / "hestia.vim")
    shutil.copy(video_rig / f"nvimrc-{variant}.lua", nvim_cfg / "init.lua")
    stage_treesitter_parsers(nvim_cfg / "parser")
    return rig


def stage_treesitter_parsers(dest):
    """Best-effort: copy any compiled Neovim treesitter parsers (parser/
    <lang>.so) already installed for the invoking user into the isolated
    rig. These are compiled binaries — this repo doesn't vendor them, so a
    machine with none produces no parser/ dir at all, and the rig's
    nvimrc-*.lua falls back to legacy :syntax cleanly (see its own
    comment) rather than erroring. Not a real dependency, just nicer
    highlighting when it's there.
    """
    candidates = list(Path.home().glob(".local/share/nvim/site/pack/*/start/nvim-treesitter/parser/*.so"))
    candidates += list(Path.home().glob(".local/share/nvim/lazy/nvim-treesitter/parser/*.so"))
    if not candidates:
        return
    dest.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    for so in candidates:
        shutil.copy(so, dest / so.name)


def load_script(path):
    with open(path) as f:
        doc = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}
    if "beats" not in doc:
        sys.exit(f"hestia-video: {path}: no 'beats' key")
    return doc


def emit_text(text, flat, rng, line_pause_range):
    """Shared by text: actions and comment:'s own typed content. Embedded
    newlines become key:Return (a human without editor autoindent typing a
    literal Enter), not a hestia-type feature — hestia-type sees plain
    text/key/pause/wpm strings. Each such synthesized Return also gets a
    trailing pause — a real coder doesn't advance to the next line at the
    same tempo as the characters within one (owner feedback, 2026-08-18:
    the typing read as too uniform, partly because line boundaries carried
    no beat at all before this).
    """
    buf = []
    for ch in text:
        if ch == "\n":
            if buf:
                flat.append(("text", "".join(buf)))
                buf = []
            flat.append(("key", "Return"))
            flat.append(("pause", str(int(rng.uniform(*line_pause_range)))))
        else:
            buf.append(ch)
    if buf:
        flat.append(("text", "".join(buf)))


def emit_command(cmd, flat):
    """A normal-mode command/motion (e.g. "3k", "4dd") typed as individual
    key: presses, deliberately NOT text:. text:'s typo injection assumes
    INSERT-mode editing (wrong char -> notice -> BackSpace -> retype);
    firing it mid-command would send an unintended vim command instead of
    a harmless typo — a mistyped digit or motion letter changes what gets
    deleted or where the cursor ends up, not just what's briefly on
    screen. key: presses are never typo'd (see hestia-type's build_argv —
    typo injection only fires inside the text: branch), so this is safe.
    """
    for ch in cmd:
        flat.append(("key", ch))


def emit_comment_line(content, leading_space, flat, rng, line_pause_range):
    """Types one /* content */ comment line — but the CLOSING */ gets
    typed immediately after the OPENING /*, before content, then the
    cursor moves back in to fill content between them. Typing the whole
    thing straight through left-to-right (the original approach) leaves
    the comment syntactically UNCLOSED for the entire time content is
    being typed — owner feedback (2026-08-19): Neovim's treesitter
    highlighting genuinely misbehaves during that window ("goes mad"),
    worse than the "just reads as plain text until closed" originally
    assumed — see docs/video-capture-design.md's comment-feature section.
    Opening-then-closing immediately is also literally how a real editor
    with bracket/comment auto-pairing behaves — see user/video-rig/*'s
    own auto-pair keymaps, added the same round for the same reason,
    extended to ()/[]/{}"" too.

    This project's C style is /* */ only, never // — confirmed against
    every existing code block in f04-writing-c (2026-08-19): even a
    comment standing alone above a data block is one self-contained
    /* ... */, never a K&R-style /* \\n * ... \\n */ continuation block.
    (/* */ is the original ISO C89/C90 comment syntax; // is a C++ import
    C didn't standardise until C99 — this curriculum teaches traditional,
    portable C, so it's the right default beyond just "matches existing
    style".) leading_space: True for inline (appended after code already
    on the line); False for block (its own line, starts at column 0, no
    auto-indent to match either — see vimrc-*/nvimrc-*'s indent notes).

    Returns the total character count typed (wrapper + content) — the
    caller needs this to compute an exact BackSpace cleanup count for
    inline comments.
    """
    wrapper = f"{' ' if leading_space else ''}/*  */"  # TWO spaces between
    emit_text(wrapper, flat, rng, line_pause_range)     # the *s — content
    flat.extend([("key", "Left")] * 3)                  # goes between them
    emit_text(content, flat, rng, line_pause_range)
    flat.extend([("key", "Right")] * 3)  # back past " */" to the true end
    return len(wrapper) + len(content)


def usable_cols(res):
    """Usable text columns for a scene's res: (e.g. "1024x768") — the real
    measured value from MEASURED_USABLE_COLS if this exact res: has been
    probed, else a rough PX_PER_COL/GUTTER_COLS estimate (see the constants'
    comment for why that estimate alone isn't trustworthy). None if res
    isn't set, in which case overflow-checking is skipped entirely (no
    known column budget to check against).
    """
    if not res:
        return None
    if res in MEASURED_USABLE_COLS:
        return MEASURED_USABLE_COLS[res]
    try:
        width_px = int(res.split("x")[0])
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        return None
    return max(10, int(width_px / PX_PER_COL) - GUTTER_COLS)


class LineColumn:
    """Tracks the CURRENT line's column position as flatten_actions
    processes actions in order — used only to decide whether an inline
    comment: would overflow the terminal's column budget (see
    emit_comment). Deliberately narrow: only text: content and an
    explicit key: Return/O/o reset or advance it. Any other key:
    (navigation, commands) leaves it unchanged — tracking arbitrary vim
    motions isn't tractable here, and in practice every comment: is
    authored right after a text: action with no navigation in between.
    """
    def __init__(self):
        self.value = 0

    def advance_text(self, text):
        for ch in text:
            self.value = 0 if ch == "\n" else self.value + 1

    def advance_key(self, key):
        if key in ("Return", "O", "o"):
            self.value = 0
        # else: unchanged — see class docstring


def comment_pacing(text, scene_wpm):
    """Comment typing ramps up with length: a long explanation is being
    read WHILE it's typed, not just during the hold after (owner,
    2026-08-19), so it doesn't need code's more deliberate pace. Hold time
    is generous and capped, not linear — a long comment's own (still
    slower-than-short-comment) typing time already gives a reader most of
    what they need; the hold is the "let it sink in" beat after typing
    stops, not the whole reading budget. typo_rate is untouched by any of
    this — it's a fixed per-character probability, so longer text already
    produces proportionally more typos for free.
    """
    chars = len(text)
    words = max(1, len(text.split()))
    wpm = min(COMMENT_WPM_MAX, scene_wpm + COMMENT_WPM_PER_CHAR * chars)
    hold_ms = min(COMMENT_HOLD_MAX_MS, COMMENT_HOLD_BASE_MS + COMMENT_HOLD_PER_WORD_MS * words)
    return wpm, hold_ms


def resolve_locale_text(text, locale):
    """A comment:'s text is either a plain string (typed identically for
    every locale — the only form any pre-2026-08-19 script uses) or a
    {locale: string} mapping — see the module docstring's Locales
    section. Falls back to DEFAULT_LOCALE's entry for any locale not yet
    present, so a script can grow locale coverage incrementally (matching
    the site's own broader per-chapter translation cadence) without ever
    blocking a render on a translation that hasn't landed yet.
    """
    if isinstance(text, str):
        return text
    if locale in text:
        return text[locale]
    if DEFAULT_LOCALE in text:
        return text[DEFAULT_LOCALE]
    sys.exit(f"hestia-video: comment text has neither {locale!r} nor {DEFAULT_LOCALE!r}: {text!r}")


def emit_comment(spec, flat, rng, line_pause_range, scene_wpm, locale, column=0, cols=None):
    """Expands one comment: action — an explanatory C comment that gets
    typed, held long enough to read, then removed, leaving the file
    byte-identical to what it would be without it. Two shapes, chosen by
    whether lines_up is present:

    - inline (a plain string, or a mapping with no lines_up): appended at
      the CURRENT cursor position (e.g. right after a line just typed) as
      " /* text */" — see emit_comment_line for why */ gets typed right
      after /*, before the content, not at the natural end — then removed
      with exactly as many BackSpace presses as characters were typed:
      insertion count == deletion count by construction, so there's no
      hand-counted BackSpace to get wrong.
    - block (a mapping WITH lines_up): its own line(s) above the code it
      explains. lines_up is how many lines above the CURRENT cursor the
      top of that code sits — the same number relativenumber shows a real
      vim/nvim user (see vimrc-*/nvimrc-*.lua — on deliberately, so a
      scene author can read this number off a real editor while writing
      the YAML). Moves up, opens a line, types the (possibly multi-line)
      comment, holds, moves to the comment's own first line, deletes
      exactly its own line count with dd, then moves back down to resume
      where the scene left off.

    An inline comment that would overflow the scene's known column budget
    (cols, from res: — see usable_cols) at its current column (column, from
    flatten_actions's LineColumn tracker) auto-converts to a block comment
    instead, word-wrapped to fit — same on-screen readability the author
    asked for inline, without the nvim soft-wrap it'd otherwise trigger at
    small resolutions. cols=None (no res: set) skips the check entirely.

    Mode matters here: an inline comment: (no lines_up) is, by this
    project's convention, ALWAYS authored mid-INSERT — right after the
    text: it annotates, cursor still live from typing. A block comment:
    (explicit lines_up) is, by convention, always authored from NORMAL
    mode (typically right after a key: Escape). Auto-conversion crosses
    that boundary — it turns an inline (insert-mode) comment into block
    styling (O/dd, which need NORMAL mode) — so it has to bracket the
    block-typing sequence with an Escape-in / A-out pair that a
    deliberately-authored block comment doesn't need (its caller already
    handles mode on both sides). Confirmed live (2026-08-19): without
    this, "O" typed as a literal capital O into the still-open INSERT
    mode instead of opening a line, corrupting the file.
    """
    spec = {"text": spec} if isinstance(spec, str) else dict(spec)
    text = resolve_locale_text(spec["text"], locale)
    is_block = "lines_up" in spec
    auto_converted = False

    if not is_block and cols is not None:
        inline_len = column + len(" /*  */") + len(text)
        if inline_len + INLINE_OVERFLOW_MARGIN > cols:
            wrap_width = max(10, cols - len("/*  */"))
            text = "\n".join(textwrap.wrap(text, width=wrap_width)) or text
            is_block = True
            auto_converted = True

    lines = text.split("\n")
    ramped_wpm, auto_hold = comment_pacing(text, scene_wpm)
    hold_ms = spec.get("hold_ms", auto_hold)

    lines_up = int(spec.get("lines_up", 0)) if is_block else 0
    if is_block:
        if auto_converted:
            flat.append(("key", "Escape"))  # insert -> normal, see docstring
        if lines_up > 0:
            emit_command(f"{lines_up}k", flat)
        flat.append(("key", "O"))

    flat.append(("wpm", str(int(ramped_wpm))))
    total_chars = 0
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        total_chars += emit_comment_line(line, not is_block, flat, rng, line_pause_range)
        if i < len(lines) - 1:
            flat.append(("key", "Return"))
            flat.append(("pause", str(int(rng.uniform(*line_pause_range)))))
    if is_block:
        # Back to normal mode BEFORE the navigate-and-delete commands below
        # — missing this sent "1k2dd2j" etc. as literal INSERT-mode text
        # instead of motions, confirmed live (2026-08-19): the block typed
        # fine, but the deletion commands showed up as garbage characters
        # in the file instead of removing anything. Inline doesn't need
        # this — its BackSpace cleanup runs WHILE still in insert mode, by
        # design (see the else branch below).
        flat.append(("key", "Escape"))
    flat.append(("wpm", str(int(scene_wpm))))
    flat.append(("pause", str(int(hold_ms))))

    if is_block:
        comment_lines = len(lines)
        if comment_lines > 1:
            emit_command(f"{comment_lines - 1}k", flat)
        emit_command(f"{comment_lines}dd", flat)
        if lines_up > 0:
            emit_command(f"{lines_up}j", flat)
        if auto_converted:
            # dd lands on the interrupted line at its first non-blank
            # column; A resumes INSERT at true end-of-line, exactly where
            # inline mode would have left the cursor — see docstring.
            emit_command("A", flat)
    else:
        flat.extend([("key", "BackSpace")] * total_chars)


def flatten_actions(doc, rng, line_pause_range, wpm, locale, cols):
    """Beats exist only for the script author's readability — flatten to
    one ordered action list. cols (see usable_cols) drives comment:'s
    inline-overflow auto-fallback via the LineColumn tracker — None skips
    that check entirely (no known column budget for this scene's res:).
    """
    flat = []
    column = LineColumn()
    for beat in doc["beats"]:
        for action in beat.get("actions", []):
            if "text" in action:
                emit_text(action["text"], flat, rng, line_pause_range)
                column.advance_text(action["text"])
            elif "comment" in action:
                # A comment: doesn't persist a column change — it's typed
                # then removed, so the line it interrupts resumes exactly
                # where it left off; only pass the CURRENT value in.
                emit_comment(action["comment"], flat, rng, line_pause_range, wpm, locale, column.value, cols)
            elif "key" in action:
                key = str(action["key"])
                flat.append(("key", key))
                column.advance_key(key)
            elif "hold" in action:
                # Not a text-editing action (no scene has used this for
                # anything but sustained game movement so far) -- deliberately
                # doesn't touch column, unlike key: above.
                spec = action["hold"]
                key = str(spec["key"])
                ms = spec["ms"]
                flat.append(("hold", f"{key}:{ms}"))
            elif "pause" in action:
                flat.append(("pause", str(action["pause"])))
            else:
                sys.exit(f"hestia-video: bad action {action!r} (want text/key/hold/pause/comment)")
    return flat


def estimate_ms(flat, wpm):
    """Rough planning estimate only — ignores jitter and hestia-type's own
    word/punctuation micro-pauses. Good enough to judge a take's length
    without a full render; not a substitute for one.
    """
    avg_char_ms = 60000 / (wpm * 5)
    total = 0.0
    for kind, val in flat:
        if kind == "text":
            total += len(val) * avg_char_ms
        elif kind == "key":
            total += 120
        elif kind == "hold":
            _, _, ms = val.rpartition(":")
            total += float(ms)
        elif kind == "pause":
            total += float(val)
    return total


def build_hook(flat, wpm, jitter, seed, drop_compensation, typo_rate):
    argv = ["hestia-type", "--wpm", str(wpm), "--jitter", str(jitter)]
    if seed is not None:
        argv += ["--seed", str(seed)]
    if drop_compensation:
        argv += ["--drop-compensation", drop_compensation]
    if typo_rate is not None:
        argv += ["--typo-rate", str(typo_rate)]
    argv += [f"{kind}:{val}" for kind, val in flat]
    return shlex.join(argv)


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("script", type=Path)
    ap.add_argument("-o", "--output", type=Path)
    ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--keep-workdir", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--res")
    ap.add_argument("--variant", choices=("dark", "light"))
    ap.add_argument("--seed", type=int)
    ap.add_argument("--locale")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    doc = load_script(args.script)

    wpm = doc.get("wpm", DEFAULT_WPM)
    jitter = doc.get("jitter")
    seed = args.seed if args.seed is not None else doc.get("seed", DEFAULT_SEED)
    drop_compensation = doc.get("drop_compensation")
    typo_rate = doc.get("typo_rate")
    line_pause_range = tuple(doc.get("line_pause_ms", DEFAULT_LINE_PAUSE_MS))
    locale = args.locale or doc.get("locale") or DEFAULT_LOCALE
    res = args.res or doc.get("res")
    cols = usable_cols(res)  # None if res isn't set — skips overflow-checking

    rng = random.Random(seed)  # drives line-pause jitter — same rng a re-run
                                 # with the same seed reproduces, consistent
                                 # with hestia-type's own seeded reproducibility
    flat = flatten_actions(doc, rng, line_pause_range, wpm, locale, cols)
    hook = build_hook(flat, wpm, jitter if jitter is not None else 0.35, seed, drop_compensation, typo_rate)

    scene = doc.get("scene", "app")
    scene_arg = doc.get("scene_arg", "bash")
    variant = args.variant or doc.get("variant") or "dark"  # hestia-shot's own default
    output = (args.output or Path.cwd() / (args.script.stem + ".mp4")).resolve()
    repo = resolve_repo()

    if args.dry_run:
        ms = estimate_ms(flat, wpm)
        print(f"# {doc.get('title', args.script.name)}")
        print(f"# locale: {locale}")
        print(f"# ~{ms/1000:.1f}s estimated ({len(flat)} actions across {len(doc['beats'])} beats)")
        for beat in doc["beats"]:
            if beat.get("label"):
                print(f"#   - {beat['label']}")
        if scene == "app":
            print("# scene_arg wrapped: env HOME=<staged rig-home> " + scene_arg)
        print(f"HESTIA_HOOK={shlex.quote(hook)} \\")
        if res:
            print(f"HESTIA_RES={shlex.quote(res)} \\")
        print(f"HESTIA_VARIANT={shlex.quote(variant)} \\")
        print(f"hestia-shot {shlex.quote(scene)} {shlex.quote(scene_arg)} {shlex.quote(str(output))}")
        return

    root = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="hestia-video."))
    # Seeded project files live in their own subdirectory, kept separate
    # from rig-home (below) so a demo that runs `ls` sees only the files
    # the scene actually seeded — not the staged rig scaffolding.
    workdir = root / "project"
    workdir.mkdir()
    try:
        for rel, content in doc.get("workdir", {}).items():
            dest = workdir / rel
            dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            dest.write_text(content)

        if scene == "app":
            # The recording-profile HOME (see stage_rig_home) — not the
            # invoking user's real one. Only the `app` scene launches a
            # raw shell/vim session that can leak operator identity; other
            # scenes (vifm/desktop/url) don't need this.
            rig_home = stage_rig_home(root, repo, variant)
            scene_arg = f"env HOME={shlex.quote(str(rig_home))} {scene_arg}"

        env = {**os.environ, "HESTIA_HOOK": hook, "HESTIA_VARIANT": variant}
        if res:
            env["HESTIA_RES"] = res

        output.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        result = subprocess.run(
            ["hestia-shot", scene, scene_arg, str(output)],
            cwd=workdir,
            env=env,
        )
        if result.returncode != 0:
            sys.exit(result.returncode)
    finally:
        if args.keep_workdir:
            print(f"hestia-video: kept workdir {root}")
        else:
            shutil.rmtree(root, ignore_errors=True)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
